@title Product Design Principles
@subtitle Fathom Bank Design Systems, for all customer-facing apps

These principles were agreed in a workshop with 26 designers and engineers on 2026-09-17. They apply to all customer-facing apps. Link to this page from every design review template.

## Clarity over cleverness

Customers should understand what a control does before they touch it. Words beat icons when money is involved, and a label that names the action removes a guess.

- Do: label buttons with the action.
- Avoid: icons without text for money movement.

## Show the money

Fees, limits and totals belong on the screen where the customer decides, not after they have committed. A fee that appears on the last screen feels like a trap, even when it was disclosed.

- Do: show fees before confirmation.
- Avoid: revealing fees on the final screen.

## Forgive mistakes

People make mistakes with money. Give them a window to reverse an action, and make the reversal as easy as the original action was.

- Do: allow undo for 10 seconds after a transfer is scheduled.
- Avoid: making cancellation a phone call.

## Accessible by default

Accessibility is a starting condition, not a retrofit. Every screen should work for customers with low vision, color blindness or a screen reader.

- Do: meet a contrast ratio of 4.5 to 1.
- Avoid: relying on color alone for status.

## Calm, not urgent

Money decisions are rarely urgent. State real deadlines plainly, and never invent time pressure to move a customer toward a choice.

- Do: state plain deadlines.
- Avoid: countdown timers that push decisions.

> If a customer has to read it twice, we wrote it wrong.
> — Ines Albrecht, Head of Design

! Disagreements with a principle go to the monthly design council, not to individual reviews.
